1
00:00:00,030 --> 00:00:07,850
Listen.

2
00:00:08,530 --> 00:00:09,030
Practice.

3
00:00:09,570 --> 00:00:09,890
Speak.

4
00:00:10,610 --> 00:00:12,440
Practice makes you perfect.

5
00:00:13,940 --> 00:00:14,520
Hey, everyone.

6
00:00:14,640 --> 00:00:15,360
Welcome back.

7
00:00:15,480 --> 00:00:19,000
Today, we're diving into effective communication, especially in business.

8
00:00:19,220 --> 00:00:20,040
Oh, this is a good one.

9
00:00:20,180 --> 00:00:26,570
We've got these conversations between two pros who really know their stuff when it comes to communication in the workplace.

10
00:00:26,690 --> 00:00:28,210
Not just like sending an email.

11
00:00:28,290 --> 00:00:28,450
Right.

12
00:00:28,650 --> 00:00:35,200
This goes deeper, like how empathy can totally change a negotiation or how just listening well can build trust.

13
00:00:35,180 --> 00:00:35,880
Exactly.

14
00:00:36,420 --> 00:00:41,980
And something that stood out to me was how they emphasize being crystal clear right from the get-go.

15
00:00:42,620 --> 00:00:48,750
They even said, and I wrote this down, express your ideas in a way that leaves absolutely no room for ambiguity.

16
00:00:48,850 --> 00:00:50,070
Yeah, that's huge.

17
00:00:50,690 --> 00:00:57,000
So many problems come from just not understanding each other, wasted time, arguments, and even morale takes a hit.

18
00:00:57,040 --> 00:01:00,300
So getting communication right, that's setting yourself up for success.

19
00:01:00,320 --> 00:01:00,700
For sure.

20
00:01:00,800 --> 00:01:02,010
So clarity is the base.

21
00:01:03,050 --> 00:01:04,890
But then what about giving feedback?

22
00:01:04,970 --> 00:01:06,110
No one loves that part, right?

23
00:01:06,370 --> 00:01:08,230
Well, they actually had a cool perspective on feedback.

24
00:01:08,290 --> 00:01:12,580
Their technique is to focus on the actions, the specific things someone did, not the person themselves.

25
00:01:12,880 --> 00:01:18,490
So not you always interrupt, but more like when I'm interrupted, it's hard to share my ideas.

26
00:01:19,190 --> 00:01:20,780
That does sound less like an attack.

27
00:01:21,020 --> 00:01:21,620
Exactly.

28
00:01:21,840 --> 00:01:29,690
It's more constructive, less likely to make someone defensive, and to make it even better, they suggest adding some positive reinforcement along with it.

29
00:01:29,950 --> 00:01:30,210
I see.

30
00:01:30,510 --> 00:01:32,090
So remind them what they're good at, too.

31
00:01:32,230 --> 00:01:33,080
Keep them motivated.

32
00:01:33,640 --> 00:01:36,120
But what about being on the receiving end?

33
00:01:36,100 --> 00:01:39,220
Feedback can be hard to swallow, even if it's meant well.

34
00:01:39,480 --> 00:01:40,020
Absolutely.

35
00:01:40,060 --> 00:01:47,730
They talk about going into any feedback situation with an open mind, see it as a chance to learn and grow, change how you think about it.

36
00:01:48,010 --> 00:01:51,500
Speaking of changing how you think, they also get into tough conversations.

37
00:01:51,800 --> 00:01:52,860
Those are unavoidable, right?

38
00:01:53,640 --> 00:01:55,200
In any workplace, you're going to have clashes.

39
00:01:55,300 --> 00:01:55,560
Right.

40
00:01:55,900 --> 00:01:58,270
And they're all about staying calm even when things get heated.

41
00:01:58,280 --> 00:02:01,030
Don't let your emotions control the whole conversation.

42
00:02:01,450 --> 00:02:02,750
That's harder than it sounds, though.

43
00:02:03,110 --> 00:02:06,210
Any practical tips on actually doing that when you feel that tension?

44
00:02:06,570 --> 00:02:08,700
They suggest, first, take a deep breath.

45
00:02:09,360 --> 00:02:12,700
Then, really try to listen and understand the other person's side.

46
00:02:13,040 --> 00:02:17,690
You don't have to agree, but seeing where they're coming from can help things cool down.

47
00:02:17,710 --> 00:02:18,870
Okay, makes sense.

48
00:02:19,550 --> 00:02:21,690
But what about when you need to say your own concerns?

49
00:02:21,770 --> 00:02:24,100
How do you do that without making it worse?

50
00:02:24,450 --> 00:02:26,090
They've got a great strategy for this.

51
00:02:26,490 --> 00:02:31,520
Instead of use statements, which can sound blamey, they say to use I statements.

52
00:02:31,640 --> 00:02:35,400
Like instead of, you're always late, try, I feel stressed when deadlines are missed.

53
00:02:35,740 --> 00:02:38,450
It's subtle, but I can see how it would make a big difference.

54
00:02:38,770 --> 00:02:40,990
It's about your experience, not pointing fingers.

55
00:02:41,510 --> 00:02:42,090
Exactly.

56
00:02:42,130 --> 00:02:44,530
Express your feelings without attacking.

57
00:02:45,160 --> 00:02:48,360
They also remind you to remember the bigger picture, the goal.

58
00:02:48,660 --> 00:02:52,340
You're trying to solve the problem, not win the argument.

59
00:02:52,730 --> 00:02:53,290
So true.

60
00:02:54,010 --> 00:02:59,620
We get caught up in the moment and forget that we're all on the same team trying to find a solution that works for everyone.

61
00:02:59,640 --> 00:03:03,090
And that actually leads to what they call the secret weapon, empathy.

62
00:03:03,630 --> 00:03:05,150
They're not just saying it's a nice feeling.

63
00:03:05,170 --> 00:03:07,790
They mean it's a tool for better communication.

64
00:03:07,850 --> 00:03:09,110
I'm listening.

65
00:03:09,410 --> 00:03:11,520
So it's more than I understand how you feel.

66
00:03:12,100 --> 00:03:14,500
How do they actually put empathy into action?

67
00:03:14,600 --> 00:03:20,030
One tip they give is to really listen and acknowledge how the other person feels, even if you disagree.

68
00:03:20,070 --> 00:03:25,340
It makes them feel heard, builds trust, and then they're more likely to listen to you too.

69
00:03:25,960 --> 00:03:26,520
That's interesting.

70
00:03:27,280 --> 00:03:28,920
Now let's tackle something else that can be tough.

71
00:03:29,740 --> 00:03:35,190
Communication barriers, language differences, cultural stuff, and of course, all the tech challenges.

72
00:03:35,250 --> 00:03:36,920
The world's a global place now.

73
00:03:37,300 --> 00:03:39,820
These professionals really stress being adaptable.

74
00:03:39,880 --> 00:03:44,470
Like, if there's a language barrier, make your language simpler to ensure the message gets through.

75
00:03:44,510 --> 00:03:45,070
Right.

76
00:03:45,150 --> 00:03:45,910
Keep it basic.

77
00:03:46,970 --> 00:03:48,550
But what about cultural differences?

78
00:03:48,890 --> 00:03:50,810
Those can be subtle and tough to navigate.

79
00:03:51,030 --> 00:03:54,880
They say take the time to actually learn about how different cultures communicate.

80
00:03:55,320 --> 00:03:56,240
Some are very direct.

81
00:03:56,560 --> 00:03:58,060
Others prefer a roundabout way.

82
00:03:58,080 --> 00:03:59,410
You've got to understand that.

83
00:03:59,490 --> 00:04:00,630
And what about technology?

84
00:04:01,350 --> 00:04:05,140
It was supposed to make things easier, but sometimes it feels like a whole new set of problems.

85
00:04:05,460 --> 00:04:06,940
They have an interesting take on that.

86
00:04:07,400 --> 00:04:15,390
While they acknowledge all the great tools like video conferencing, they actually say to prioritize face-to-face communication whenever you can.

87
00:04:15,410 --> 00:04:16,240
Really?

88
00:04:16,280 --> 00:04:17,520
I wouldn't have guessed that.

89
00:04:17,840 --> 00:04:19,520
Why is in-person so important to them?

90
00:04:19,600 --> 00:04:25,650
They believe it builds stronger connections because you pick up on those little nonverbal cues that get lost virtually.

91
00:04:25,670 --> 00:04:26,830
It builds trust better too.

92
00:04:27,270 --> 00:04:28,940
They actually said... Let me grab my notes.

93
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:29,620
I don't want to miss this.

94
00:04:29,800 --> 00:04:37,930
They said, while virtual meetings are convenient, face-to-face communication fosters stronger connections and allows for better nonverbal cues.

95
00:04:39,430 --> 00:04:40,850
I think that sums it up nicely.

96
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:45,270
So even those quick chats in the hallway or by the coffee machine can be crucial.

97
00:04:45,410 --> 00:04:45,950
Exactly.

98
00:04:45,990 --> 00:04:47,570
It's not just about formal meetings.

99
00:04:47,670 --> 00:04:50,280
It's about those moments where you build that real connection.

100
00:04:51,100 --> 00:04:53,560
Speaking of nonverbal cues, that's another thing they get into.

101
00:04:53,580 --> 00:04:54,380
I'm all about that.

102
00:04:55,370 --> 00:04:56,750
Body language is fascinating.

103
00:04:57,190 --> 00:05:00,700
How it can say so much about what someone's truly thinking.

104
00:05:00,860 --> 00:05:07,420
They talk about how your posture, your facial expressions, even the tone of your voice speak volumes, especially at work.

105
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:12,530
Being aware of your own is important, but they also say you've got to pay attention to others' nonverbal cues, too.

106
00:05:13,210 --> 00:05:19,330
So if someone says they're fine, but their body's screaming stressed out, you need to notice that disconnect.

107
00:05:19,440 --> 00:05:20,200
Exactly.

108
00:05:20,240 --> 00:05:23,890
They're big on reading those subtle signals to get the real meaning behind the words.

109
00:05:24,430 --> 00:05:27,160
That's valuable anywhere, but especially at work.

110
00:05:27,980 --> 00:05:35,590
And with so many virtual meetings now where you don't see as much, being mindful of your tone and choosing your words carefully is even more important.

111
00:05:35,570 --> 00:05:36,030
I see.

112
00:05:36,430 --> 00:05:40,680
But interpreting those nonverbal cues across cultures, that can get tricky, right?

113
00:05:40,700 --> 00:05:41,180
Oh, for sure.

114
00:05:41,240 --> 00:05:46,530
Eye contact, gestures, how close you stand, all different meanings in different cultures.

115
00:05:46,990 --> 00:05:47,870
Do your research.

116
00:05:48,350 --> 00:05:49,990
Be sensitive to what's normal for them.

117
00:05:50,330 --> 00:05:51,010
Great advice.

118
00:05:51,760 --> 00:05:54,160
Any other surprising things about nonverbal communication?

119
00:05:54,400 --> 00:05:56,480
They brought up something I hadn't thought much about.

120
00:05:56,520 --> 00:05:59,330
Using silence strategically.

121
00:05:59,630 --> 00:06:00,210
Silence.

122
00:06:00,510 --> 00:06:01,350
Now that's interesting.

123
00:06:01,470 --> 00:06:02,070
Tell me more.

124
00:06:02,170 --> 00:06:06,870
They say silence can be powerful, especially when you feel like you have to fill the space with words.

125
00:06:07,630 --> 00:06:10,160
Allowing a pause gives space to think.

126
00:06:10,180 --> 00:06:10,600
It's true.

127
00:06:11,320 --> 00:06:16,460
We rush to fill those silences sometimes, but quiet moments can make a conversation more meaningful.

128
00:06:16,900 --> 00:06:22,210
Being comfortable with those pauses and realizing they can be as powerful as words, that's the key.

129
00:06:22,590 --> 00:06:23,730
Okay, I'll try to remember that.

130
00:06:24,550 --> 00:06:28,820
Now I'm curious, what nonverbal cues do you personally pay attention to?

131
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:29,580
Hmm.

132
00:06:29,900 --> 00:06:32,200
For me, eye contact's a big one.

133
00:06:32,580 --> 00:06:34,430
Shows they're engaged, sincere.

134
00:06:34,470 --> 00:06:36,090
And posture, too.

135
00:06:36,130 --> 00:06:37,310
That can reveal a lot.

136
00:06:37,790 --> 00:06:39,570
Closed off means uncomfortable, maybe.

137
00:06:39,600 --> 00:06:43,960
But open posture usually means they're confident and open to what you're saying.

138
00:06:44,060 --> 00:06:44,640
Those are good ones.

139
00:06:44,900 --> 00:06:48,710
And I think it reminds all of us to be more aware of how our own body language comes across.

140
00:06:48,990 --> 00:06:49,510
Absolutely.

141
00:06:49,550 --> 00:06:50,510
It goes both ways.

142
00:06:50,710 --> 00:06:52,710
Be aware of yours and how you read other people's.

143
00:06:52,950 --> 00:06:53,150
All right.

144
00:06:53,170 --> 00:06:56,040
Let's switch gears now and talk about a fundamental part of communication.

145
00:06:56,820 --> 00:06:57,320
Listening.

146
00:06:57,550 --> 00:07:02,840
They're adamant that listening isn't just hearing words, it's actively engaging with what's being said.

147
00:07:03,260 --> 00:07:08,480
So more than just nodding and saying, uh-huh, what are some ways to get better at truly listening?

148
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:10,520
They say, first, be present.

149
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:11,750
Put your phone away.

150
00:07:12,120 --> 00:07:13,030
Close your laptop.

151
00:07:13,210 --> 00:07:14,450
Focus on the conversation.

152
00:07:14,650 --> 00:07:16,150
Second, your body language matters.

153
00:07:16,550 --> 00:07:19,690
Make eye contact, nod, use your posture to show you're interested.

154
00:07:19,890 --> 00:07:22,070
And lastly, ask clarifying questions.

155
00:07:22,190 --> 00:07:24,640
It shows you're not just waiting for your turn to talk.

156
00:07:24,780 --> 00:07:26,380
So be mentally and physically present.

157
00:07:26,560 --> 00:07:27,340
Exactly.

158
00:07:27,480 --> 00:07:30,740
And listen to the emotions too, not just the words themselves.

159
00:07:31,040 --> 00:07:34,770
Pay attention to tone, facial expressions, get a sense of how they feel.

160
00:07:34,970 --> 00:07:35,190
Right.

161
00:07:35,610 --> 00:07:39,050
And they say mastering this kind of listening can really build trust at work.

162
00:07:39,370 --> 00:07:42,490
When people feel heard and understood, it creates a bond.

163
00:07:42,600 --> 00:07:43,130
Makes sense.

164
00:07:43,310 --> 00:07:46,420
It builds respect and makes working together so much easier.

165
00:07:46,660 --> 00:07:51,000
And they even have advice for when someone's taking over the conversation others can't get a word in.

166
00:07:52,060 --> 00:07:52,700
Those meetings.

167
00:07:53,160 --> 00:07:54,200
What do they say to do?

168
00:07:54,350 --> 00:07:57,040
They say gently interrupt and redirect.

169
00:07:57,220 --> 00:07:58,920
Make sure everyone gets their chance to speak.

170
00:07:59,300 --> 00:08:00,650
So find that balance.

171
00:08:01,090 --> 00:08:02,590
Be assertive but respectful.

172
00:08:03,210 --> 00:08:04,450
Everyone's voice matters.

173
00:08:04,680 --> 00:08:05,200
Exactly.

174
00:08:05,240 --> 00:08:06,360
Make it a fair discussion.

175
00:08:06,540 --> 00:08:09,460
You know, my weakness is staying focused when I'm trying to listen.

176
00:08:10,170 --> 00:08:11,190
So many distractions.

177
00:08:11,210 --> 00:08:12,070
We've all been there.

178
00:08:12,350 --> 00:08:14,880
But the more you practice being mindful, the better you get at it.

179
00:08:14,920 --> 00:08:15,180
All right.

180
00:08:15,260 --> 00:08:15,790
Good advice.

181
00:08:16,470 --> 00:08:21,610
Now, let's talk about something that can be a bit of a puzzle, adapting to different communication styles.

182
00:08:21,870 --> 00:08:23,430
We all have our own way of talking, right?

183
00:08:23,790 --> 00:08:24,710
Some people get to the point.

184
00:08:25,060 --> 00:08:26,200
Others need all the details.

185
00:08:26,640 --> 00:08:31,680
And they really stress that you have to understand those differences and adapt your style to match.

186
00:08:31,700 --> 00:08:35,530
So be flexible and change your approach based on who you're talking to.

187
00:08:35,750 --> 00:08:36,270
Exactly.

188
00:08:36,530 --> 00:08:38,130
Figure out how they like to communicate.

189
00:08:38,590 --> 00:08:39,890
Are they brief or detailed?

190
00:08:40,510 --> 00:08:41,590
Direct or subtle?

191
00:08:42,270 --> 00:08:44,040
Then adjust how you talk to them.

192
00:08:44,240 --> 00:08:45,460
But how do you figure that out?

193
00:08:45,540 --> 00:08:47,020
Just watch how they communicate.

194
00:08:47,340 --> 00:08:48,160
That's a great start.

195
00:08:48,400 --> 00:08:51,170
Listen to their words, tone, body language.

196
00:08:51,590 --> 00:08:53,740
Do they jump right in or go around the subject?

197
00:08:53,750 --> 00:08:55,080
Jokes or serious?

198
00:08:55,480 --> 00:08:57,080
You become a communication detective.

199
00:08:57,160 --> 00:08:57,740
I like that.

200
00:08:58,260 --> 00:09:00,000
Gather clues and adjust your approach.

201
00:09:00,630 --> 00:09:05,350
They even give specific tips for certain types of people, like with executives.

202
00:09:05,450 --> 00:09:06,090
Be concise.

203
00:09:06,310 --> 00:09:07,170
Focus on the results.

204
00:09:07,270 --> 00:09:08,030
No long stories.

205
00:09:08,290 --> 00:09:08,860
Get to the point.

206
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:09,700
Right.

207
00:09:09,720 --> 00:09:11,420
Executives are busy, want results.

208
00:09:11,540 --> 00:09:13,260
They like things clear and to the point.

209
00:09:13,420 --> 00:09:13,700
Got it.

210
00:09:14,040 --> 00:09:15,440
What about new people on the team?

211
00:09:15,460 --> 00:09:16,100
Be patient.

212
00:09:16,140 --> 00:09:16,780
Explain more.

213
00:09:17,140 --> 00:09:18,680
New folks need more background.

214
00:09:18,750 --> 00:09:19,710
So be thorough.

215
00:09:20,130 --> 00:09:20,750
That makes sense.

216
00:09:21,070 --> 00:09:24,990
And of course, cultural differences are huge, especially now with global teams.

217
00:09:25,190 --> 00:09:25,750
Oh, yeah.

218
00:09:26,210 --> 00:09:28,100
They say cultural awareness is key.

219
00:09:28,620 --> 00:09:33,520
Learning how different cultures communicate can prevent a lot of problems and help you build relationships.

220
00:09:33,770 --> 00:09:34,190
For sure.

221
00:09:34,730 --> 00:09:37,990
And even when we're trying our best, sometimes people just don't get it.

222
00:09:38,170 --> 00:09:39,880
They're big on feedback to fix that.

223
00:09:40,460 --> 00:09:44,340
Don't be afraid to ask if they understood or if something needs clarifying.

224
00:09:44,860 --> 00:09:46,810
So it's not just about giving the information.

225
00:09:46,830 --> 00:09:48,630
It's about making sure they actually get it.

226
00:09:48,790 --> 00:09:49,210
Bingo.

227
00:09:49,810 --> 00:09:50,690
It's a two-way street.

228
00:09:51,280 --> 00:09:53,380
sending and receiving the information properly.

229
00:09:53,700 --> 00:09:54,840
Well, this has been eye-opening.

230
00:09:54,880 --> 00:09:56,710
It feels like a good place to take a break.

231
00:09:57,110 --> 00:10:03,110
We've covered so much from being clear and empathetic to all those nonverbal cues and becoming a better listener.

232
00:10:03,250 --> 00:10:04,430
And there's so much more to come.

233
00:10:04,810 --> 00:10:16,910
Stay tuned for part two of our deep dive into effective communication, where we'll look at building strong relationships, dealing with tough conversations, and how to use technology without losing that human connection.

234
00:10:17,130 --> 00:10:17,650
See you soon.

235
00:10:18,190 --> 00:10:19,010
Welcome back.

236
00:10:19,500 --> 00:10:23,900
Last time we were talking about how important it is to be adaptable with your communication style.

237
00:10:24,260 --> 00:10:30,470
Right, like figuring out if you're dealing with an executive who wants the bottom line or someone new who needs all the details.

238
00:10:30,710 --> 00:10:36,260
And this whole adapting thing, it's really crucial when you're trying to build those strong relationships at work.

239
00:10:36,280 --> 00:10:41,790
These professionals, they talk about trust being so important, like the foundation of a good working relationship.

240
00:10:41,810 --> 00:10:44,470
And they say open communication is how you build it.

241
00:10:44,570 --> 00:10:48,100
Okay, so it's more than just sounding good in meetings.

242
00:10:48,120 --> 00:10:52,410
It's about those everyday interactions that show you're reliable and can be trusted.

243
00:10:53,320 --> 00:10:55,860
How do you actually do that through communicating?

244
00:10:56,320 --> 00:10:58,120
One thing they say is to be consistent.

245
00:10:58,400 --> 00:11:00,940
Regular check-ins, keep people in the loop, that kind of thing.

246
00:11:01,540 --> 00:11:04,930
Prevents those annoying mix-ups and make sure everyone's on the same page.

247
00:11:04,950 --> 00:11:08,890
So be proactive, keep those lines open, instead of waiting until things go wrong.

248
00:11:09,050 --> 00:11:09,570
Exactly.

249
00:11:09,610 --> 00:11:11,200
And be responsive, too.

250
00:11:11,240 --> 00:11:18,220
Replying to emails without making people wait, acknowledging when someone reaches out, it shows you respect their time and what they have to say.

251
00:11:18,560 --> 00:11:19,830
Keeps things moving smoothly.

252
00:11:20,310 --> 00:11:24,870
We talked about feedback before, but they bring it up again for building relationships.

253
00:11:25,480 --> 00:11:26,420
It's tricky, right?

254
00:11:26,520 --> 00:11:29,550
You want to help but not sound like you're just criticizing.

255
00:11:30,150 --> 00:11:30,410
It is.

256
00:11:30,550 --> 00:11:31,490
It's a balancing act.

257
00:11:31,670 --> 00:11:32,030
Yeah.

258
00:11:32,050 --> 00:11:36,280
But they say feedback, when it's done right, actually shows respect.

259
00:11:36,700 --> 00:11:38,350
You're invested in that person's growth.

260
00:11:38,500 --> 00:11:38,910
Okay.

261
00:11:38,930 --> 00:11:42,890
So how do you make sure it's helpful and not just picking them apart?

262
00:11:43,480 --> 00:11:49,690
They say focus on what they did, specific actions, and combine anything critical with some genuine praise.

263
00:11:49,870 --> 00:11:54,150
So you're not a team player, but more like, hey, I noticed you didn't share your thoughts in that last meeting.

264
00:11:54,450 --> 00:11:57,400
Your ideas are great, and I think the team would really benefit from hearing them.

265
00:11:57,680 --> 00:11:58,200
Exactly.

266
00:11:58,560 --> 00:12:01,280
You recognize what they're good at while pointing out an area to work on.

267
00:12:01,700 --> 00:12:06,310
And don't underestimate a little appreciation, saying thank you, noticing someone's effort.

268
00:12:06,330 --> 00:12:08,730
Those little things can really strengthen the bond.

269
00:12:08,810 --> 00:12:11,010
Create a culture where people feel valued.

270
00:12:11,460 --> 00:12:15,600
Besides feedback and saying thanks, anything else for building strong relationships.

271
00:12:16,180 --> 00:12:17,200
They talk about helping out.

272
00:12:17,800 --> 00:12:22,690
Being the person who lends a hand, shares what they know, it creates a feeling of partnership.

273
00:12:23,070 --> 00:12:26,440
So that sense of we're in this together.

274
00:12:26,720 --> 00:12:26,940
Right.

275
00:12:27,180 --> 00:12:29,680
And sometimes the simplest thing is the most powerful.

276
00:12:30,020 --> 00:12:30,760
Just listen.

277
00:12:31,480 --> 00:12:35,730
Really listen to what the other person is saying without interrupting or jumping to conclusions.

278
00:12:35,950 --> 00:12:36,810
Active listening.

279
00:12:37,490 --> 00:12:38,210
There it is again.

280
00:12:38,250 --> 00:12:41,730
It really seems to be the key for pretty much everything.

281
00:12:43,050 --> 00:12:48,280
Speaking of communication, they had some interesting things to say about how technology has changed things at work.

282
00:12:48,330 --> 00:12:49,630
It's a mixed bag, isn't it?

283
00:12:49,670 --> 00:12:56,580
We have all these tools, email, instant messaging, video calls, but sometimes it's overwhelming and you lose that human connection.

284
00:12:56,820 --> 00:12:57,260
For sure.

285
00:12:57,560 --> 00:13:01,990
I've definitely felt like I'm communicating constantly, but not really connecting with anyone.

286
00:13:02,110 --> 00:13:04,030
They say pick the right tool for the job.

287
00:13:04,250 --> 00:13:09,630
Email for formal stuff, instant message for quick things, video for when you need that face-to-face.

288
00:13:09,710 --> 00:13:10,750
Be strategic about it.

289
00:13:10,790 --> 00:13:12,370
And set boundaries, right?

290
00:13:12,530 --> 00:13:13,840
Don't let tech run your life.

291
00:13:14,180 --> 00:13:16,620
Make sure you're still making time for real conversations.

292
00:13:16,600 --> 00:13:17,260
Absolutely.

293
00:13:17,560 --> 00:13:24,100
They also point out that with tech, you often miss those little nonverbal cues that can completely change how you understand something.

294
00:13:24,300 --> 00:13:24,500
Yeah.

295
00:13:24,610 --> 00:13:27,410
Tone in an email or text is so easy to misread.

296
00:13:27,850 --> 00:13:28,330
Exactly.

297
00:13:28,430 --> 00:13:30,370
So they say be extra clear in writing.

298
00:13:30,750 --> 00:13:34,930
And if you need a nuanced discussion or want to build that rapport, get on a video call.

299
00:13:35,190 --> 00:13:37,440
And even with tech, have some ground rules.

300
00:13:37,460 --> 00:13:45,640
So like agree on how quickly you'll reply to emails or when it's OK to just message instead of scheduling a call gets everyone on the same page.

301
00:13:45,880 --> 00:13:52,760
Now, let's talk about something a bit tougher, those difficult conversations, whether it's conflict with a coworker or giving hard feedback.

302
00:13:53,230 --> 00:13:53,970
We all have them.

303
00:13:54,330 --> 00:13:56,730
And these professionals had some really good advice.

304
00:13:57,210 --> 00:13:57,790
I'm all ears.

305
00:13:58,530 --> 00:14:02,720
I think we could all use some tips on how to handle those gracefully and make them productive.

306
00:14:02,920 --> 00:14:06,080
First thing, stay calm even when you feel like exploding.

307
00:14:06,380 --> 00:14:07,160
Easier said than done.

308
00:14:07,800 --> 00:14:10,250
How do you actually pull that off when someone's pushing your buttons?

309
00:14:10,360 --> 00:14:17,250
They say, take a step back, literally walk away, breathe and remind yourself you're aiming for a solution, not winning a fight.

310
00:14:17,380 --> 00:14:17,960
OK, good point.

311
00:14:18,260 --> 00:14:21,540
Sometimes you just want to prove you're right, but that's not helpful in the long run.

312
00:14:22,020 --> 00:14:22,640
What else?

313
00:14:22,660 --> 00:14:24,050
Active listening is huge here.

314
00:14:24,210 --> 00:14:26,470
Really hear their side before you react.

315
00:14:26,810 --> 00:14:33,800
So not just planning your response while they're talking, but actually trying to understand their point of view, even if you disagree.

316
00:14:33,840 --> 00:14:34,120
Right.

317
00:14:34,500 --> 00:14:39,860
And then when you say your piece, be clear and respectful, no blaming or attacking.

318
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:41,260
Tough when you're frustrated, though.

319
00:14:41,430 --> 00:14:41,730
It is.

320
00:14:41,930 --> 00:14:44,950
But remember, focus on the problem, not the person.

321
00:14:44,990 --> 00:14:48,600
They suggest I statements instead of you statements.

322
00:14:48,820 --> 00:14:52,400
Like, I feel stressed when deadlines are missed instead of you're always late.

323
00:14:52,680 --> 00:14:53,210
Perfect.

324
00:14:53,750 --> 00:14:55,890
It's your experience, not an accusation.

325
00:14:56,530 --> 00:14:58,250
And empathy is really important here, too.

326
00:14:58,410 --> 00:15:03,560
Even if you don't agree, understanding their side and acknowledging their feelings can calm things down.

327
00:15:03,820 --> 00:15:07,870
It's about finding that common ground, remembering we're all human, even when we disagree.

328
00:15:08,370 --> 00:15:13,120
And in the end, the goal is to find a solution that works, not to win the argument.

329
00:15:13,480 --> 00:15:15,100
So collaborate, find a win-win.

330
00:15:15,280 --> 00:15:17,000
They say it's not about winning.

331
00:15:17,160 --> 00:15:20,070
It's about moving forward in a way that works for everyone.

332
00:15:20,110 --> 00:15:24,600
Now, they also talk about communicating across cultures, which has its own challenges.

333
00:15:24,740 --> 00:15:24,920
Right.

334
00:15:25,020 --> 00:15:29,610
Different cultures have different ways of communicating, and that can easily lead to misunderstandings.

335
00:15:29,630 --> 00:15:31,170
They say awareness is key.

336
00:15:31,460 --> 00:15:33,880
Take the time to learn how different cultures communicate.

337
00:15:34,450 --> 00:15:35,730
So do your research.

338
00:15:35,870 --> 00:15:37,730
Be sensitive to what's normal for them.

339
00:15:37,970 --> 00:15:38,490
Exactly.

340
00:15:38,950 --> 00:15:40,340
Some cultures are very direct.

341
00:15:40,380 --> 00:15:42,280
Others like to hint and be subtle.

342
00:15:42,400 --> 00:15:43,780
You need to know that going in.

343
00:15:43,800 --> 00:15:45,260
So what about body language?

344
00:15:45,280 --> 00:15:47,500
I bet that's a minefield across cultures.

345
00:15:47,780 --> 00:15:48,300
Big time.

346
00:15:48,900 --> 00:15:52,710
Eye contact, gestures, personal space, all different.

347
00:15:53,190 --> 00:15:56,710
Pay attention to your own body language and be observant of theirs.

348
00:15:56,970 --> 00:16:00,580
So be aware of what you're doing and how you're reading their signals, right?

349
00:16:00,760 --> 00:16:01,320
You got it.

350
00:16:02,060 --> 00:16:03,880
And be patient, flexible.

351
00:16:04,350 --> 00:16:06,110
Don't assume you're always reading them right.

352
00:16:06,650 --> 00:16:09,400
And be willing to adapt your style to make everyone comfortable.

353
00:16:09,620 --> 00:16:11,640
It's about being curious and open to learning.

354
00:16:11,900 --> 00:16:15,730
And building that rapport, that trust is super important across cultures.

355
00:16:16,230 --> 00:16:18,400
Respect their culture and how they communicate.

356
00:16:18,440 --> 00:16:19,780
That goes a long way.

357
00:16:19,950 --> 00:16:20,790
All great points.

358
00:16:21,270 --> 00:16:28,760
It makes me think about being more intentional with communication in general, whether it's cultural differences or just someone who has a different style than you.

359
00:16:28,780 --> 00:16:32,390
Be aware of what you're saying, how you're saying it, and be open to what they're saying, too.

360
00:16:32,870 --> 00:16:35,120
It's about building connection, understanding.

361
00:16:35,180 --> 00:16:39,770
Speaking of understanding, let's get back to a skill we touched on before, listening.

362
00:16:39,970 --> 00:16:40,590
I'm all ears.

363
00:16:41,210 --> 00:16:44,320
We talked about active listening, but I want to hear more about what they have to say on that.

364
00:16:44,340 --> 00:16:49,630
They're really clear that listening isn't just passively letting words go in one ear and out the other.

365
00:16:49,690 --> 00:16:51,690
It takes effort, focus.

366
00:16:51,710 --> 00:16:54,010
You have to really want to get what the other person is saying.

367
00:16:54,110 --> 00:16:57,560
So be in the moment, fully engaged with what they're saying.

368
00:16:58,240 --> 00:17:01,970
What are some actual things you can do to become a better listener?

369
00:17:02,180 --> 00:17:03,520
They have a few suggestions.

370
00:17:04,110 --> 00:17:06,250
First, ditch the distractions.

371
00:17:06,750 --> 00:17:11,000
Phone away, laptop closed, create a space where you can actually focus on the conversation.

372
00:17:11,600 --> 00:17:13,540
Second, your body language matters.

373
00:17:13,940 --> 00:17:17,170
Make eye contact, nod, sit in a way that shows you're listening.

374
00:17:18,010 --> 00:17:20,890
And third, ask questions to make sure you're understanding.

375
00:17:21,250 --> 00:17:23,400
So be present mentally and physically.

376
00:17:23,620 --> 00:17:24,180
Exactly.

377
00:17:24,660 --> 00:17:26,100
And don't just listen to the words.

378
00:17:26,640 --> 00:17:28,970
Pay attention to the emotions behind them too.

379
00:17:29,100 --> 00:17:33,810
So listen for tone of voice, watch their face, try to get a sense of how they're feeling.

380
00:17:34,170 --> 00:17:34,550
That's it.

381
00:17:35,150 --> 00:17:39,500
And they say when you master this kind of listening, it builds trust in relationships.

382
00:17:39,700 --> 00:17:43,450
People feel heard, understood, and it creates a stronger bond.

383
00:17:43,750 --> 00:17:50,180
It creates that feeling of respect, empathy, which are so important for communication to really work.

384
00:17:50,480 --> 00:17:56,770
And they even have some strategies for those situations where one person dominates the conversation and nobody else can get a word in.

385
00:17:57,010 --> 00:17:58,170
Oh, I've been in those meetings.

386
00:17:58,970 --> 00:18:01,310
So frustrating when you feel like your voice doesn't matter.

387
00:18:01,710 --> 00:18:02,590
What do they suggest?

388
00:18:03,010 --> 00:18:06,410
They say politely but firmly interrupt and redirect.

389
00:18:06,670 --> 00:18:08,840
Make sure everyone gets a chance to talk.

390
00:18:08,860 --> 00:18:10,880
So be assertive but respectful.

391
00:18:11,320 --> 00:18:13,840
You're looking out for everyone, not shutting anyone down.

392
00:18:13,860 --> 00:18:14,080
Exactly.

393
00:18:14,100 --> 00:18:17,140
You're making it a balanced conversation where everyone can contribute.

394
00:18:17,190 --> 00:18:20,660
My biggest struggle with listening, staying focused.

395
00:18:20,680 --> 00:18:21,740
Distractions are everywhere.

396
00:18:21,860 --> 00:18:22,660
We all feel that.

397
00:18:23,000 --> 00:18:25,880
But the more you practice being mindful, the better you get at it.

398
00:18:26,200 --> 00:18:26,480
All right.

399
00:18:26,780 --> 00:18:27,340
Good advice.

400
00:18:28,220 --> 00:18:33,890
Now let's dive into something that can feel really tricky, adapting to different communication styles.

401
00:18:34,730 --> 00:18:36,170
We all have our own way of talking, right?

402
00:18:36,910 --> 00:18:38,070
Some people are straight to the point.

403
00:18:38,090 --> 00:18:39,570
Others like all the details.

404
00:18:40,080 --> 00:18:44,720
And they really hammer home that you need to understand those differences and adjust how you communicate.

405
00:18:44,820 --> 00:18:48,810
So be flexible, change your approach based on who you're talking to.

406
00:18:48,910 --> 00:18:49,130
Right.

407
00:18:49,690 --> 00:18:51,430
Pay attention to how they like to communicate.

408
00:18:51,990 --> 00:18:54,760
Are they concise or do they want the whole story?

409
00:18:54,800 --> 00:18:57,290
Are they direct or more subtle?

410
00:18:57,990 --> 00:18:59,290
Then adjust how you talk to them.

411
00:18:59,650 --> 00:19:03,620
So you have to be a bit of a communication detective, figure out their style.

412
00:19:03,750 --> 00:19:04,410
I like that.

413
00:19:05,150 --> 00:19:07,240
Gather the clues and adapt your approach.

414
00:19:07,700 --> 00:19:10,420
They even give specific tips for certain types of people.

415
00:19:10,880 --> 00:19:14,150
Like with executives, keep it brief and focus on results.

416
00:19:14,390 --> 00:19:16,310
No long explanations, just the bottom line.

417
00:19:16,490 --> 00:19:16,630
Yep.

418
00:19:17,210 --> 00:19:18,100
Executives are busy.

419
00:19:18,160 --> 00:19:18,900
They want results.

420
00:19:19,040 --> 00:19:23,530
So they appreciate clear, concise communication that focuses on the outcome.

421
00:19:24,190 --> 00:19:24,590
Makes sense.

422
00:19:24,910 --> 00:19:27,190
What about when you're communicating with new people on the team?

423
00:19:27,460 --> 00:19:30,340
Then they suggest being more patient and explaining things more.

424
00:19:30,860 --> 00:19:34,590
New folks need more context and guidance, so be clear and thorough.

425
00:19:35,170 --> 00:19:35,910
Okay, that makes sense.

426
00:19:36,210 --> 00:19:40,080
And of course, cultural differences play a huge role, especially in global teams these days.

427
00:19:40,340 --> 00:19:41,860
They're big on cultural awareness.

428
00:19:42,380 --> 00:19:44,840
Take the time to learn how different cultures communicate.

429
00:19:44,900 --> 00:19:48,730
It can prevent a lot of problems and help you build stronger relationships.

430
00:19:48,970 --> 00:19:49,390
For sure.

431
00:19:49,870 --> 00:19:56,230
And sometimes, even when you're trying your best to communicate clearly, misunderstandings still happen.

432
00:19:56,330 --> 00:19:58,860
They're big on using feedback to fix that.

433
00:19:59,280 --> 00:20:03,080
Don't be afraid to ask if they understood or if you need to clarify something.

434
00:20:03,300 --> 00:20:05,820
So communication isn't just about sending the information.

435
00:20:06,280 --> 00:20:09,070
It's about making sure it was actually received and understood.

436
00:20:09,420 --> 00:20:09,870
You got it.

437
00:20:10,110 --> 00:20:10,990
It's a two-way street.

438
00:20:11,890 --> 00:20:14,190
Sending and receiving information properly.

439
00:20:14,430 --> 00:20:15,750
Well, this has been eye-opening.

440
00:20:15,770 --> 00:20:17,110
It feels like a good place to take a break.

441
00:20:17,130 --> 00:20:22,580
We've covered so much from being clear and empathetic to those nonverbal cues and becoming a better listener.

442
00:20:22,780 --> 00:20:23,980
And there's so much more to come.

443
00:20:24,870 --> 00:20:36,240
Stay tuned for part three of our deep dive into effective communication, where we'll look at building stronger relationships, dealing with tough conversations, and how to use technology without losing that human connection.

444
00:20:36,260 --> 00:20:36,680
See you soon.

445
00:20:37,050 --> 00:20:40,070
Okay, I'm ready to put those active listening skills into practice.

446
00:20:41,230 --> 00:20:51,160
It's interesting how they emphasize that listening is so crucial in the workplace, not just for building those relationships, but for problem solving and even giving feedback.

447
00:20:51,210 --> 00:20:56,140
Which actually reminds me, they had some really insightful things to say about feedback itself, didn't they?

448
00:20:56,160 --> 00:20:56,680
Oh, they did.

449
00:20:56,800 --> 00:20:58,500
It comes up a lot in these conversations.

450
00:20:58,840 --> 00:21:04,330
They see feedback as essential, like truly essential for growth, both at work and just in life.

451
00:21:04,590 --> 00:21:09,150
It's a gift, helps you see your blind spots, get better at things, and be more effective.

452
00:21:09,930 --> 00:21:12,610
But giving feedback well, that can be tricky.

453
00:21:12,810 --> 00:21:15,580
You want to help but not sound like you're just criticizing.

454
00:21:15,860 --> 00:21:16,900
Oh, yeah, it's a fine line.

455
00:21:17,500 --> 00:21:19,380
But they have some great tips for getting it right.

456
00:21:19,680 --> 00:21:24,150
First, they say focus on specific things the person did, not their personality.

457
00:21:24,690 --> 00:21:30,640
So instead of you're disorganized, it's more like I noticed the reports last week had some formatting inconsistencies.

458
00:21:30,980 --> 00:21:31,680
Be specific.

459
00:21:31,740 --> 00:21:32,420
Give examples.

460
00:21:32,660 --> 00:21:32,880
Right.

461
00:21:33,060 --> 00:21:36,270
And they talk about offering solutions, not just pointing out problems.

462
00:21:36,310 --> 00:21:39,490
So give them something to work with, a way to actually improve.

463
00:21:39,520 --> 00:21:41,230
And be mindful of their feelings.

464
00:21:41,890 --> 00:21:45,030
Even when you're giving tough feedback, be empathetic and respectful.

465
00:21:45,190 --> 00:21:45,690
Makes sense.

466
00:21:46,210 --> 00:21:48,480
And they talk about receiving feedback well too, right?

467
00:21:48,800 --> 00:21:51,340
Being open to it, seeing it as a way to learn.

468
00:21:51,820 --> 00:21:55,150
Yeah, it's not always fun to hear criticism, even if it's meant well.

469
00:21:56,110 --> 00:21:58,550
But try to see it as a gift, a chance to get better.

470
00:21:59,170 --> 00:22:02,920
And remember, the person giving it probably wants you to succeed.

471
00:22:03,440 --> 00:22:07,710
Now, another thing they talked about, which is often overlooked, is nonverbal communication.

472
00:22:07,940 --> 00:22:13,310
Ah, yes, body language, facial expressions, tone of voice, all that unspoken stuff.

473
00:22:13,410 --> 00:22:18,850
They talk about how those nonverbal cues can be so powerful, sometimes even stronger than what you actually say.

474
00:22:18,870 --> 00:22:22,680
They say, especially at work, be aware of your own body language.

475
00:22:23,280 --> 00:22:31,270
Posture, eye contact, your tone, even subtle things can say a lot about how confident you are, how engaged you are, what your intentions are.

476
00:22:31,550 --> 00:22:33,170
So think about how you're presenting yourself.

477
00:22:33,550 --> 00:22:35,350
Make sure it matches what you're trying to say.

478
00:22:35,550 --> 00:22:38,670
And pay attention to other people's nonverbal cues, too.

479
00:22:38,840 --> 00:22:43,690
Pick up on those little hints that tell you how they really feel, even if their words are saying something different.

480
00:22:43,950 --> 00:22:47,760
They talk about how sometimes your words and your body language don't match up.

481
00:22:47,950 --> 00:22:52,020
Like someone says, I'm fine, but everything else screams, I'm stressed.

482
00:22:52,040 --> 00:22:56,770
So you've got to pay attention to that mismatch and try to understand the whole message, not just the words.

483
00:22:56,790 --> 00:22:57,130
Right.

484
00:22:57,250 --> 00:23:01,080
And they say, make sure your own words and body language are telling the same story.

485
00:23:01,180 --> 00:23:02,840
Otherwise, people get confused.

486
00:23:02,860 --> 00:23:04,100
They don't trust what you're saying.

487
00:23:04,260 --> 00:23:04,760
Makes sense.

488
00:23:05,230 --> 00:23:08,560
Do they give any tips on using nonverbal communication effectively?

489
00:23:08,800 --> 00:23:09,180
Oh, yeah.

490
00:23:09,220 --> 00:23:09,880
They have a bunch.

491
00:23:10,020 --> 00:23:13,280
Like maintain good eye contact to show you're engaged.

492
00:23:13,820 --> 00:23:19,830
Use gestures to emphasize things and try mirroring the other person's body language a little to build rapport.

493
00:23:20,190 --> 00:23:22,170
So even small things can make a difference.

494
00:23:22,540 --> 00:23:24,600
And be aware of cultural differences, too.

495
00:23:24,840 --> 00:23:27,980
What's polite in one culture might be rude in another.

496
00:23:28,160 --> 00:23:28,480
True.

